WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.683
A sensor analysis job estimates the average reading
across two partitions.

00:00:04.683 --> 00:00:10.568
One holds ten readings averaging twenty, and the
other holds ninety readings averaging forty.

00:00:10.568 --> 00:00:16.327
Averaging the two local averages gives thirty, but
the correct global mean is thirty-eight.

00:00:16.327 --> 00:00:22.655
Each worker must return both a sum and a count: two
hundred plus three thousand six hundred, divided

00:00:22.655 --> 00:00:23.604
by one hundred.

00:00:23.604 --> 00:00:29.007
The summary must preserve the information needed to
combine unequal partitions correctly.

00:00:29.007 --> 00:00:34.775
Compare that with finding the global minimum: each
worker needs to send only its local minimum.

00:00:34.775 --> 00:00:38.903
The amount and meaning of intermediate data depend
on the operation.

00:00:38.903 --> 00:00:44.367
Sending all raw readings to one coordinator may be
correct, but can destroy the benefit of

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.703
distributing the work.

00:00:45.704 --> 00:00:51.472
Design the summary, estimate transfer volume and
then identify where combination happens.

00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:56.980
Some calculations require multiple communication
rounds or richer intermediate state.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:03.461
More workers reduce only the parallel portion of the
cost; data movement and coordination still need

00:01:03.461 --> 00:01:06.248
an explicit place in the performance model.

00:01:06.248 --> 00:01:11.835
A transport agency wants total delay minutes by
route from a finite day of trip records.

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:15.709
Each mapper emits the route identifier and that
trip's delay.

00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:20.026
The shuffle groups values by route, and each reducer
sums its group.

00:01:20.026 --> 00:01:25.741
The programmer specifies meaningful transformations
while the framework arranges tasks and

00:01:25.741 --> 00:01:27.138
intermediate movement.

00:01:27.148 --> 00:01:33.331
Following one route through those stages makes the
key's role concrete: it determines which records

00:01:33.331 --> 00:01:34.955
must meet for aggregation.

00:01:34.955 --> 00:01:40.702
If one route has most of the trips, its reducer may
dominate completion time even when other

00:01:40.702 --> 00:01:42.076
reducers finish early.

00:01:42.076 --> 00:01:48.135
Local combining can reduce transfer for a sum, but
the design still needs to account for skew and

00:01:48.135 --> 00:01:48.697
recovery.

00:01:48.697 --> 00:01:54.983
A framework that retains reusable intermediate
datasets in memory can help repeated analyses, while

00:01:54.983 --> 00:01:59.364
its lineage or persistence rules determine recovery
after lost state.

00:01:59.364 --> 00:02:01.713
These are bounded batch computations.

00:02:01.713 --> 00:02:08.062
They do not by themselves specify event-time windows
or handling of an endless stream, so keep those

00:02:08.062 --> 00:02:13.078
separate requirements visible when comparing batch
and streaming architectures.

00:02:13.097 --> 00:02:19.005
Stream processing reacts to an ongoing sequence
rather than waiting for a complete dataset.

00:02:19.005 --> 00:02:25.498
Monitoring and recommendations may benefit from
short update latency, while historical reporting can

00:02:25.498 --> 00:02:27.576
tolerate larger batch intervals.

00:02:27.576 --> 00:02:30.952
Low latency is a target, not an automatic guarantee.

00:02:30.952 --> 00:02:37.250
A stream operator may still wait for enough
evidence, and overloaded consumers can accumulate
lag

00:02:37.250 --> 00:02:40.301
even when the architecture is called real time.
